"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,599\nCues and context\n\n4\n00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:16,403\nDivers learned lists of words either on a beach or under water,\n\n5\n00:00:16,713 --> 00:00:19,941\nthen recalled them in the same place or in the other.\n\n6\n00:00:20,461 --> 00:00:23,139\nRecall was best when the two settings matched.\n\n7\n00:00:23,481 --> 00:00:26,921\nWords learned under water came back better under water.\n\n8\n00:00:27,091 --> 00:00:29,835\nThe principle is called encoding specificity.\n\n9\n00:00:30,292 --> 00:00:33,330\nA cue helps only if it was part of what got stored.\n\n10\n00:00:33,916 --> 00:00:36,767\nWhen people learned a word alongside one cue,\n\n11\n00:00:37,023 --> 00:00:41,817\nthat cue brought it back better than another word that seemed more obviously related.\n\n12\n00:00:41,987 --> 00:00:43,920\nHow big is the effect of place?\n\n13\n00:00:44,358 --> 00:00:48,817\nA meta-analysis of ninety three effects found it small but reliable,\n\n14\n00:00:49,112 --> 00:00:52,128\nabout point two eight of a standard deviation.\n\n15\n00:00:52,588 --> 00:00:55,035\nIt shrinks when other good cues are present,\n\n16\n00:00:55,363 --> 00:00:58,861\nand when people picture the original setting before recalling.\n\n17\n00:00:59,032 --> 00:01:01,295\nContext matters most for recall.\n\n18\n00:01:01,698 --> 00:01:04,877\nWhen divers were instead asked to recognise the words,\n\n19\n00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:06,955\nthe setting made no difference.\n\n20\n00:01:07,125 --> 00:01:08,689\nVarying the setting can help.\n\n21\n00:01:09,232 --> 00:01:16,584\nPeople who studied a list in two different rooms recalled more in a new room than people who studied twice in the same room.\n\n22\n00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:17,548\nA caution.\n\n23\n00:01:17,964 --> 00:01:19,960\nChanging the room is a weak lever,\n\n24\n00:01:20,095 --> 00:01:22,395\nand some studies find no effect at all.\n\n25\n00:01:22,883 --> 00:01:26,520\nWhat matters more is whether the right cues are present at test,\n\n26\n00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:28,886\ninside the material itself.\n\n27\n00:01:29,056 --> 00:01:29,820\nFor teaching.\n\n28\n00:01:30,323 --> 00:01:33,421\nPractise retrieval in more than one setting and format,\n\n29\n00:01:33,636 --> 00:01:37,172\nso knowledge does not depend on one room or one slide.\n\n30\n00:01:37,735 --> 00:01:39,065\nWhen students are stuck,\n\n31\n00:01:39,279 --> 00:01:41,032\nhave them picture where they learned it.\n\n32\n00:01:41,434 --> 00:01:43,957\nAnd build meaningful cues into the material,\n\n33\n00:01:43,977 --> 00:01:45,841\nlike questions and categories,\n\n34\n00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:48,207\nthat will still be there at the exam.\n\n35\n00:01:48,377 --> 00:01:50,837\nMemory is not stored in isolation.\n\n36\n00:01:51,201 --> 00:01:53,844\nIt comes back through the cues it was stored with.\n\n"
